------------------------------------------------------------------------------
phase G: narrowing the step phase F put between 4 KiB and 8 KiB
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  EXPLORATORY, and a NARROWING rather than a new question. Phase F reads a
  flat 0.998 cycles/instruction from a 64-instruction loop body through 1024
  and 1.251 at 2048, which locates a boundary in that octave and nothing
  finer. This set adds ONE intermediate, compiled against a 1024-instruction
  body in the SAME kernel so the comparison does not cross a build.

  WHAT THIS CAN AND CANNOT SAY. It locates a boundary in LOOP-BODY SIZE. It
  is not a cache capacity: no document in the ISA documentation or either
  vendor tree gives an instruction cache size or a miss cost, and a step in
  cost against footprint is equally consistent with a prefetch window, a
  TLB-like structure or an L1 access pattern. Narrowing the boundary does
  not license a different noun for it.

  --gset 4 of 0..4 is compiled in; run the other sets to fill the row.

  variant   thr      g_1024  probe     bytes cyc/instr        step
  t1        1         1.401  g_1408   5632 B    1.401      +0.000

  A STEP at the intermediate puts the boundary at or below that many bytes
  of loop body; a FLAT pair puts it above, with phase F's 8192 bounding it
  from the other side. Read the FIVE --gset runs together: they bracket it
  between footprints that were actually run, and that bracket is the whole
  claim. Sets 3 and 4 (4608 and 5632 B) were added on 2026-08-09 because the
  onset was bracketed only to (4096, 5120] and neither endpoint of that
  bracket had ever been measured from INSIDE it.

